# ComposeUX Lottie Animation Widget [SPEC:CXC-LOTTIE]

> A widget that plays a Lottie (Bodymovin) vector animation, realizing [CXC-R-DRAW-LOTTIE]. It renders through Skia's own Lottie module (skottie) on the workhorse tier — so it is pixel-consistent by construction — degrades to a baked sprite sheet on the CYD, and exposes playback controls that bind to the bus the same way any attribute does: state on the bus, motion in the widget.

---

## 1. Intent

### [CXC-LOT-INTENT] Vector Motion Without Frames on the Bus

- definition: The LottieWidget plays a vector animation defined by a Lottie JSON document. Its use is non-interactive motion — a dradis sweep, a pulsing alert glyph, a spinner, a transition flourish.
- key-insight: Lottie is the *pure-Skia* animation primitive — **skottie** is Skia's own Lottie renderer, so it composites through the same backend as everything else and needs no external decoder (contrast [CXC-RM-MEDIA], which does). Playback is driven by bus state, never by streaming frames.
- status: OPERATIONAL on the Skia tier (2026-07-07) — `render/widgets/lottie.py` + `render/lottie/` package; C1–C5, C7 PASS; C6 (CYD sprite sheet) deferred to M5. NOTE the renderer-seam change record below: skottie is NOT bound by skia-python, so the Skia tier currently renders via the engine's Canvas-contract Lottie evaluator (Skia still rasterizes every pixel).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-LOTTIE]

---

## 2. Rendering Per Tier

### [CXC-LOT-RENDER] One Source, Three Renderers

- skia-tier (macOS / RPi / MinnowBoard): render via **skottie** (skia-python's Skottie module). The engine advances the animation to a logical time and skottie draws that frame into the Skia surface — pixel-consistent across the tier by construction.
- skia-tier-as-built (2026-07-07): skia-python does NOT bind skottie (see change record); the tier renders via `render/lottie/` — a zero-dependency Lottie-SUBSET evaluator emitting Canvas-contract calls, behind a documented renderer seam (`render_lottie()` / `RENDERER`). Skia rasterizes every pixel, so [CXC-R-C1] identity holds; a future skottie-capable build swaps in behind the seam without touching widget/bindings/tests.
- browser: **skottie ships inside CanvasKit** — so under Decision 1 Option A (Pyodide + CanvasKit) the browser plays the same animation nearly for free; a TS re-port would need lottie-web instead (a point for Option A).
- CYD: skottie is too heavy for the ESP32 at runtime, so the CYD plays a **pre-rendered sprite sheet** ([CXC-LOT-CYD]) — design-identical frames baked from the same Lottie.
- test: the same Lottie at the same logical time renders design-identically on the Skia tier and (as sprite frames) on the CYD.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-LOTTIE]

### [CXC-LOT-DETERMINISM] Deterministic Frame Sampling

- constraint: like state-driven behaviors ([SPEC:CXC-BIND]), Lottie playback is time-based; for pixel-goldens it must sample deterministically — render the animation at a fixed logical time (reuse `RenderApp.render_frame_at(t)`), so golden frames reproduce byte-identically macOS↔RPi.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-LOTTIE]

---

## 3. Bus-Bindable Playback

### [CXC-LOT-PLAYBACK] Playback Controls Are Just Bindings

- role: The player exposes a control surface that binds to namespaces/variables via the [SPEC:CXC-BIND] mechanism — the same "state on the bus, behavior in the widget" model, applied to animation transport.
- controls (each literal or namespace-bound): `autoplay` (bool), `loop` (bool | count), `speed` / `setSpeed` (float multiplier), `goToAndStop` (frame or `0..1` progress — jump-and-hold), `playSegments` (`[a, b]` frame/marker range to play/loop). Marker names from the Lottie resolve to frames.
- dradis: the sweep is a Lottie whose `speed` (or `goToAndStop` progress) is bound to a `scanning`/heading namespace — the needle position is bus-driven, the interpolation is engine-owned.
- test: publishing to the bound `speed` namespace changes playback rate within one frame; `goToAndStop` bound to a value holds the matching frame; `playSegments` loops the named range.
- as-built (2026-07-07): .compose.yaml attributes are snake_case (`speed`, `autoplay`, `loop`, `go_to_and_stop`, `play_segments`); the parser normalizes lottie-web aliases (`setSpeed`/`goToAndStop`/`playSegments`). Registered in `BINDABLE_ATTRS_BY_TYPE['lottie']` so the Composer Bindings tab surfaces the transport with zero editor changes ([CXC-BE-INTROSPECT]). `play_segments` binds via the object form only (a bare string is a marker-name literal). Transport mutations rebase the playhead against the engine's logical clock (`on_clock_tick`, the new runtime clocked-widget registry) — pure function of the clock, per [CXC-LOT-DETERMINISM]. Widgets may declare `coerce_bound_attr(attr, raw)` for binding coercion the generic type rules cannot infer (engine/bindings.py hook, added this pass).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-LOTTIE]

### [CXC-LOT-ASSET] Lottie Source Is a Bus Asset

- role: the animation JSON is referenced as `src: doc://…/x.json` or `bus://assets/…/x.json`, fetched via the asset manager (`render/engine/assets.py`) with caching. Embedded images inside the Lottie resolve through the same asset path.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-LOTTIE]

---

## 4. CYD Sprite-Sheet Pipeline

### [CXC-LOT-CYD] Bake, Don't Decode

- role: `composeio push` pre-renders the Lottie (via skottie, offline) into a **sprite sheet** stored in document_store ([CXC-R-C18]); the CYD firmware plays the frame sequence. Frame count/size are bounded for the ESP32 (PSRAM budget); a `fps`/`frames` cap is declared per animation.
- degradation: bound playback controls that the sprite-sheet can honor (play/pause/loop, goToAndStop to a baked frame) work; continuous `setSpeed` maps to nearest baked frame rate. Segment/marker playback maps to baked frame ranges.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-LOTTIE]

---

## 5. Compliance

| ID | Requirement | Testable Criterion |
|----|-------------|-------------------|
| CXC-LOT-C1 | Skia playback | A Lottie plays on the Skia tier; frame at time t is deterministic and pixel-identical macOS↔RPi ([CXC-R-C17]) |
| CXC-LOT-C2 | Bindable speed | `speed` bound to a namespace changes the play rate within one frame |
| CXC-LOT-C3 | goToAndStop | `goToAndStop` bound to a value holds the matching frame |
| CXC-LOT-C4 | Segments | `playSegments [a,b]` plays/loops exactly the named range |
| CXC-LOT-C5 | Dradis demo | A dradis Lottie whose sweep is namespace-driven renders the needle at the bound position |
| CXC-LOT-C6 | CYD sprite sheet | `composeio push` bakes a sprite sheet; the CYD plays the frame sequence ([CXC-R-C18]) |
| CXC-LOT-C7 | Asset fetch | The Lottie JSON loads from doc://` / `bus://` via the asset manager |

- results (2026-07-07): C1 PASS (8/8 sampled dradis frames SHA-256-identical macOS↔RPi5, `tests/render/captures/lottie/*/manifest.json`; goldens `lottie_*` regression-pinned; whole-demo composition incl. engine text also SHA-identical) · C2 PASS (rate change lands on the very next sampled frame, playhead rebases continuously — no jump) · C3 PASS (frame + 0..1 progress forms; release resumes from held frame) · C4 PASS (frame pairs, marker names, "a,b" strings; range never exits [a,b)) · C5 PASS (`render/examples/dradis_lottie.compose.yaml`, markers sweep/calibrate; Intelligence-over-MQTT smoke 5/5 BOTH platforms `smoke_lottie_intelligence_mqtt.py`) · C6 DEFERRED to M5 (needs the embedded config-driven boot; design retained in [CXC-LOT-CYD]) · C7 PASS (doc:// base64 payloads + bus:// via AssetManager, plus a LIVE bus_storage round-trip: `bus://assets/animations/dradis_lottie.json`). Suites: 276 green macOS, 272+4skip RPi5; live window 58fps macOS with a mid-run bound-speed change applied.
- compliance-tests: `tests/render/test_lottie.py` (25).

