## Change Records

- change: "Skia-tier renderer = the engine's Canvas-contract Lottie evaluator (`render/lottie/`), NOT skottie (2026-07-07, build pass) — a DEPENDENCY DECISION RATIFIED by David 2026-07-07; the widget/bindings/tests are renderer-agnostic behind the `render_lottie()` seam."
- context_for_change:
  - prior_state: "The spec named skottie (skia-python's Skottie module) as the Skia-tier renderer, 'pixel-consistent by construction'."
  - reasoning: "Verified 2026-07-07: skia-python does NOT bind skottie — upstream issue kyamagu/skia-python#57 open since 2020, zero skottie symbols in the shipped m144 binaries on BOTH macOS-arm64 and linux-aarch64 (the deployed wheels), and no newer wheel exists. The kickoff's sanctioned fallback was 'a Lottie evaluator rasterizing through the Canvas contract'; the natural parser dependency (python-lottie, PyPI `lottie`) is AGPLv3+ — license-incompatible with ComposeIO's open-reuse posture — so the evaluator is a zero-dependency hand-written Lottie-SUBSET (shapes/groups/transforms/keyframes with bezier easing/markers/gradients/parenting/precomp; unsupported features warn-once and skip). Scene evaluation is pure IEEE-754 arithmetic with fixed-iteration bezier solves; Skia rasterizes every pixel through the same backend as all widgets, so cross-platform identity is preserved — proven byte-exact (8/8 SHA-256) macOS↔RPi5."
  - rejected_alternative: "(a) python-lottie as a dependency — rejected on AGPLv3+. (b) rlottie bindings — rejected: rasterizes outside the Canvas contract, breaking the one-rasterizer pixel gate. (c) Building a skottie-capable skia-python fork now — deferred, not rejected: it means owning a patched pybind11 binding + custom Skia builds for macOS-arm64/linux-aarch64/x86_64 indefinitely, for a widget whose scope is non-interactive motion."
  - counter_argument: "A subset evaluator will never play arbitrary After-Effects exports (mattes, trim paths, effects, text layers) the way skottie would."
  - counter_response: "The spec's own scope is non-interactive motion (sweeps/pulses/spinners/flourishes — [CXC-LOT-INTENT]); the subset covers it and degrades honestly (warn-once, screen never broken). The renderer seam means adopting a skottie build later is a swap, not a rewrite — and the same custom-Skia lift would ALSO be what strict byte-identity for stroked content requires (see the determinism record), so the two decisions merge into one future build investment."
  - invalidating_assumption: "That skia-python remains skottie-less and that Lottie usage stays within non-interactive motion. If designers need arbitrary AE exports (mattes/effects), or upstream ships Skottie bindings, reopen and swap the seam."
  - implication: "`render/lottie/RENDERER = 'canvas-contract'` names the active implementation. The CYD sprite-sheet baker ([CXC-LOT-CYD], M5) can reuse the same evaluator offline — design-identical frames without a Skia dependency on the build host."

- change: "Determinism finding + authoring rule (2026-07-07): STROKED cubic contours (e.g. stroked circles) can diverge ±1 on isolated pixels between the macOS-arm64 and linux-aarch64 skia-python builds; FILLS are bit-exact. Circular rings in determinism-gated Lottie assets are authored as annulus FILLS (paired `el` with the inner reversed, `d: 3`)."
- context_for_change:
  - prior_state: "[CXC-BIND-C6] established byte-identical sampled frames macOS↔RPi5, on flat/rect content. First Lottie captures failed strict SHA-256 (1–5 px per frame, Δ=1) while passing tolerance goldens."
  - reasoning: "Bisected to a single primitive: a stroked large-radius cubic-bezier circle under a translated CTM (e.g. r=100 at +12,+12, width 1.5 or 2.0) rasterizes with ±1 differences on a handful of AA pixels; the same geometry at the origin, all gradient fills (linear, radial, alpha-stop), path fills, annulus fills at any offset, and straight-line strokes are byte-identical. The engine's Lottie evaluator emits bit-identical path commands on both platforms (verified) — the divergence is inside Skia's stroker/AA pipeline across differently-compiled wheel builds (compiler FMA/rounding), i.e. a Skia-build property that would affect skottie identically."
  - rejected_alternative: "(a) Loosen [CXC-LOT-C1] to tolerance-only — rejected: byte-identity is achievable and was achieved by removing the divergent primitive from the asset. (b) Engine-side deterministic stroker (convert strokes to fill outlines) — deferred: real scope (caps/joins/curve offsetting) for a case authoring avoids."
  - counter_argument: "Asset-side authoring rules are fragile — any third-party Lottie with stroked circles will fail strict identity."
  - counter_response: "Third-party stroked content still passes the tolerance goldens (Δ≤1 on <0.01% of pixels) — visually indistinguishable; strict SHA identity is the DEMO/golden-pinning bar, and hand-authored deterministic assets are already this spec's norm (license posture). The `d: 3` annulus idiom is standard Lottie, not a private extension."
  - invalidating_assumption: "That both tiers run differently-built upstream skia-python wheels. A single pinned self-built Skia (the same investment a skottie binding needs) would make stroked content bit-exact too — if that build ever lands, this authoring rule relaxes to a note."
  - implication: "`scripts/generate_dradis_lottie.py` regenerates the flagship asset reproducibly with annulus rings; the renderer supports `el.d == 3` reversed winding. Result: 8/8 sampled frames + the full demo composition SHA-256-identical macOS↔RPi5."

